    <section class="legal-block" id="tracking">
      <span class="legal-n">08</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Cookies, tracking, and who else touches the data</h2>
        <p>No advertising. No fingerprinting. No third-party tracker that runs on its own. Two things can record anything about you, and both are named below: Microsoft Clarity, session replay and heatmaps of the studio, and Rewardful, the partner programme's referral cookie on this website. Each exists only where a deployment is set up with it, each loads only after you allow its category, and every word on a Clarity replay is masked before it leaves your browser — the <a href="#cookies" data-cookies>Cookies</a> link in the footer of every page is where you answer, and where you take the answer back.</p>

        <h3>This website</h3>
        <p>It sets <strong>no cookie of its own</strong>. It keeps three things in your own browser, and they never leave it:</p>
        <ul class="legal-list">
          <li><code>pps-consent</code> — your answer to the cookie notice, with the date you gave it, so we can show you were asked and so we stop asking. Kept 12 months, then we ask again.</li>
          <li><code>prospektor.scan</code> — the scan you asked for, carried from the front page into the checkout form so you don't fill the same thing in twice. Gone when you close the tab.</li>
          <li><code>prospektor.goal</code> — the sentence you typed about what you want the studio to find, so a reload doesn't lose it. Gone when you close the tab.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Netlify Real User Metrics</strong> is the optional one, and the only measurement on this site that keeps anything about a single visit. It puts nothing on your device — no cookie, no browser storage of any kind — but as you leave a page it posts that page's load timings, along with your IP address, to Netlify, who host this site. <strong>It does not run unless you allow it.</strong> The tag is taken out of the page before the page reaches you, and your consent is the only thing that puts it back.</p>
        <p><strong>The homepage test.</strong> The homepage may show one of two versions of its four tiles, picked at random each time the page loads. For each version we count page views, and how many of them press the scan button, open the demo or start sign-up, as daily totals. Nothing about you is stored, on your device or with us. The page sends only which version it showed and what was pressed, we keep only the day's totals, and the links into the studio carry the version's name and nothing else. Because nothing is put on your device, the cookie notice does not list it.</p>
        <p><strong>Rewardful</strong> is the partner programme's referral cookie, and it is switched off: nothing about it is served until we turn the programme on. Once it is on, a page you reach through a partner's link can set one first-party cookie, <code>rewardful.referral</code>, holding Rewardful's id for that referral (a random identifier, never your name or address) so that if you then buy a workspace the partner is credited. It is kept 60 days. <strong>It does not run unless you allow marketing</strong> in the cookie notice, which asks you again on the day it appears; until you do, the script is not loaded, the cookie is not set, and Rewardful hears nothing from your browser. The referral id, and only that, goes to Stripe with your purchase so Rewardful can match the sale to the partner.</p>

        <h3>The studio</h3>
        <p>The studio sets three cookies, all of them functional:</p>
        <ul class="legal-list">
          <li><code>pps_session</code> — signed, HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Lax, expires after 12 hours. Your name, email and picture URL sit inside the cookie itself; there's no session database behind it.</li>
          <li><code>pps_oauth_state</code> — 10 minutes, protects the sign-in against cross-site request forgery.</li>
          <li><code>pps_share</code> — 10 minutes, remembers which share link sent you to the sign-in page.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The last two are cleared as soon as sign-in finishes. It keeps seven more things in your browser; six of them never leave it, and the first is also written to your account while you are signed in:</p>
        <ul class="legal-list">
          <li><code>pps-theme</code> — this device's light/dark choice, and when you made it. While you are signed in it is also written to your account profile, so your other devices start the same way; nobody else reads it, and nothing in the product is decided by it. Until you clear site data.</li>
          <li><code>pps-consent</code> — your answer to the studio's own cookie notice, with the date. 12 months, then it asks again.</li>
          <li><code>pps-example-gone</code> — that you removed the example pitch from your library, so it stays removed. Until you clear site data.</li>
          <li><code>pps-language</code>: the language this device last read the studio in, so the next visit loads its words at once. Absent for English. Until you clear site data.</li>
          <li><code>pps-steps-skipped</code>: which Next steps you pressed Skip on, so they stay hidden on this device. Until you clear site data.</li>
          <li><code>pps-report</code>: which week's report this device has already seen, and which of its blockers you marked not relevant, so neither comes back on this device. Until you clear site data.</li>
          <li><code>pps-draft</code> — what you have typed into a box and not yet sent, so a reload gives it back. It lives in that one tab and is forgotten the moment you send it or close the tab.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>And one thing that is not a cookie of ours and is off unless you switch it on: <strong>Microsoft Clarity</strong>, session replay and heatmaps of the studio, so we can see where it confuses people. It runs only on a deployment that names a Clarity project, only after you allow analytics in the studio's cookie notice, and with every word on the screen masked before a replay leaves your browser, so nothing you research, nobody's name and no draft you are writing reaches Microsoft. Its own cookies, which tell one session from the next, are kept a year. Withdraw at the Cookies link and nothing more is sent.</p>
        <p>That is the whole list, on both properties, and it is the same list the code works from: the studio's own <a href="https://studio.prospektor.ai/#cookies">Cookies</a> link shows it, item by item, and is where you change your answer there.</p>

