                <td>Only for a workspace whose owner pasted a HighLevel Private Integration token and their sub-account's Location ID, and only when a member of that workspace presses <strong>Push to HighLevel</strong> on one finished pitch. At connect we make one read and no writes: we ask HighLevel for the sub-account's name, which we keep so the settings card can say whose it is (a token that may not read it is proven instead by reading one contact, which names nobody). The token travels as a request header, and nothing else about the workspace goes with it. That press writes into the customer's own HighLevel: for each named decision-maker we found a work email address for, up to ten, through one of the contact-data providers above, their first and last name, that address, the target company's name and website, the tag <em>prospektor</em> and the source <em>Prospektor</em>, as contacts (a person we have no address for is not created at all, and is named in the note instead); when nobody has an address, one contact named for the company, with its name and website; and one note on the first of those contacts holding the date, the fit score and verdict, the one thing, the value angle, the names and roles of the people, a link back to the pitch in the studio, and up to twelve of the sources the run read. Each press adds one note. No opportunity is opened: one needs a pipeline and a stage of the customer's own. It is also read from, so that a second push finds what the first one wrote: a person is looked up by email address with HighLevel's own duplicate check, and nothing of ours is written onto a contact that was already there. Never the drafted deliverables, never a LinkedIn URL or a phone number, nothing about the person pressing, no session, no IP address: the calls are made from our server. The customer controls their own sub-account; we write into it on their press. It is HighLevel, Inc., based in the United States.</td>
                <td>Pushing a finished pitch into a customer's own CRM</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Sendblue</td>
                <td>Only if you ticked the box on a free run — the same tick that keeps your address. What goes to Sendblue is one contact: your mobile number, your email address, the domain you ran, the link to your own run, the word <em>free-run</em> and the date you ticked. Never a message from us — Sendblue is the texting service product news by text is sent from, and it holds the contact so that such a text can reach you; the studio itself sends no text. It is Round One Inc., trading as Sendblue, based in the United States. Untick the box and nothing goes; withdraw the text permission, clear your number or ask us to erase you and the contact is deleted there too. Reply STOP to any text and the texts stop.</td>
                <td>Holding the mobile numbers that product news by text is sent to</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>TypeSafe AI</td>
                <td>Only while we have this second reader switched on, and only from the box on the studio's home screen (<em>How can Prospektor help you today?</em>): the one sentence a member types there, at most 600 characters, together with that workspace's brief and its standing hunt lines, so it can say which part of the studio that sentence is asking for. It answers with one of those parts and how sure it is. It writes nothing, opens no web page, and is told nothing about the person: no name, no address, no session, no IP address. This is the same material the box already sends to Anthropic, read a second time by a second company, and the answer the member sees is still Anthropic's. What we keep is a scoreboard: the hour, which workspace, what each of the two readers picked and how long each took, never the sentence and never the brief. Its own privacy policy, read 19 September 2026, says it will not train or fine-tune any model on what it is sent, and will not pass it on to anyone but its own service providers. It is Typesafe AI, Inc., based in the United States.</td>
                <td>A second reading of the sentence typed into the studio's home box, measured against the reading that answers it</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p><strong>We do run marketing email.</strong> If you have an account, or ticked the box when you asked for a free run, we send product news, new features and occasional offers alongside the transactional mail — your receipt, your welcome, your sign-in. Today it goes through our email provider above and nowhere else, and we do not track whether a message was opened or which links were clicked. Product news by text, to the people who ticked the box on a free run and gave a mobile number, is sent from Sendblue, in the table above; every text can be stopped by replying STOP. If we add a newsletter you can subscribe to, it will go through a newsletter platform, which will be named in the table above — and that kind of platform does count opens and clicks, so we will say so here before the first one goes out. <strong>Every marketing message carries a one-click unsubscribe</strong>, and unsubscribing never stops the transactional mail, because that is how having an account works.</p>
        <p>Not used, so you can rule them out: no advertising vendor, and no session recording except Microsoft Clarity as described under the studio above, only with your consent. The CRMs in the table are the customer's own, written into on their press &mdash; nothing about you goes to a CRM of ours, because we don't have one.</p>
        <p>Anthropic, Netlify, Google, Clearbit, Clay, Tomba, treg, Moltsets, Stripe, Rewardful, Postmark, Slack, HubSpot, Affinity, Close, Reevo, Attio, HighLevel, Sendblue and TypeSafe AI all process data outside the European Economic Area, mainly in the United States (Rewardful in Canada, Attio in the United Kingdom); Findymail processes only inside it, and Lightfield publishes no processing location, so it sits on neither side. That's an international transfer, and it needs a documented safeguard &mdash; standard contractual clauses, or each provider's own data-processing terms. We haven't completed that documentation. Ask us and we'll tell you where it stands.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 09 -->
    <section class="legal-block" id="retention">
      <span class="legal-n">09</span>
      <div>
        <h2>How long we keep it — and what "delete" actually does</h2>
        <p><strong>Almost nothing expires on its own.</strong> There is exactly one retention limit in the product, and it is the rolling ninety-day window on messages read in from a customer's own Slack (<a href="#network">section 06</a>). Everything else has none: there is no automatic deletion anywhere, and a run from January is still there in December unless somebody removed it.</p>
        <p>What a user can do from inside the studio:</p>
        <ul class="legal-list">
          <li><strong>Archive a pitch</strong> — hidden from the list, fully recoverable.</li>
          <li><strong>Delete a pitch</strong> — removed from storage.</li>
          <li><strong>Revoke a share link</strong> — stops working immediately for everyone, including people who already opened it. The share record and its list of viewers are kept, stamped with the time of revocation.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Two things that catch people out:</p>
        <ul class="legal-list">
          <li>Deleting a pitch doesn't delete everything derived from it. Drafts a user edited may have been kept as brand-voice examples so future runs sound like that team, and those examples outlive the pitch. It's deliberate — un-learning a writing style is its own decision — but it means "delete the pitch" is not "delete everything".</li>
          <li>Share links can carry an expiry date in the underlying API, but nothing in the interface sets one. Every link created today stays open until someone revokes it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A reading of a website from the front-page field is its own case, because it is filed under the domain rather than under an account: it is kept indefinitely like everything else, we will delete one on request, and deleting it does not stop a new one being made if somebody types that address in again. <a href="#scan">Section 05</a> is the full answer.</p>
        <p>There is <strong>no self-service account deletion and no export button.</strong> Both exist only as a person doing it by hand, on request. We'd rather say that than imply a button that isn't there.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 10 -->
    <section class="legal-block" id="rights">
      <span class="legal-n">10</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Your rights, and how to use them</h2>
        <p>If you're in the UK or EU, you have the right to see what we hold about you, correct it, have it deleted, object to us holding it at all, get a copy in a portable form, and complain to your national data protection authority. We apply the same to everyone wherever they are, because running two standards is more work than running one.</p>
        <p>To use any of them, email <a href="mailto:{{ site.privacyEmail }}">{{ site.privacyEmail }}</a>. Tell us what you want and enough to find you: for <a href="#research">section 04</a> or <a href="#network">section 06</a>, your name and employer, since those are the only handles we have on you; for <a href="#viewers">section 07</a> or an account, the email address you signed in with; for <a href="#scan">section 05</a>, the web address itself, because a reading is filed under the domain and under nothing else. If we can't identify you from that, we'll ask for more rather than refuse.</p>
        <p>What then actually happens: a person goes and does it by hand. We'll confirm when it's done, and we'll come back to you within a month at the outside — normally much sooner. Where the data sits inside a client's workspace, we'll act on it and tell that client.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 11 -->
    <section class="legal-block" id="separation">
      <span class="legal-n">11</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Keeping workspaces apart</h2>
        <p>Every stored record is namespaced by client, and the workspace is resolved fresh from your signed-in email on every single request. One client's data isn't addressable from another client's session, and removing somebody from a client's allowlist takes effect immediately rather than at their next sign-in. That behaviour is covered by automated tests.</p>
        <p>Signing in at all needs one of three things: being on a client's email allowlist, an invitation to create a workspace, or a live share link. A share link gets you sight of one pitch — every other part of the studio refuses it. Speaker notes, compliance findings and the internal progress log never leave the workspace they were made in.</p>
        <p>No system is perfect, and we'd rather describe what we actually do than promise it can't be broken.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

